CAT = "iespRisk"; SEG = "RSK"
# Units 4 and 8 of Safe Pass: introduction to risk assessment, behaviour-based safety.
# Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 s. 19 and Schedule 3 (general principles of prevention);
# HSA Safe System of Work Plan (SSWP).
Q = [
('On a site, an unguarded floor opening is an example of what?',
 'A hazard', ['A risk rating', 'A control measure', 'A method statement'],
 'A hazard is anything with the potential to cause harm, such as an open edge or a live cable. The risk is how likely and how serious the harm would be.'),
('What is meant by risk?',
 'The likelihood and severity of harm', ['Any object found on a building site', 'The money an accident would cost', 'The list of rules on the site board'],
 'Risk combines how likely it is that someone will be harmed by a hazard and how bad the harm could be. Controls aim to bring it down.'),
('Which section of the 2005 Act requires employers to identify hazards and assess the risks?',
 'Section 19', ['Section 2', 'Section 40', 'Section 77'],
 'Section 19 requires every employer to identify the hazards in the place of work, assess the risks and have a written risk assessment.'),
('Under section 19 of the 2005 Act, what must the employer do after identifying the hazards?',
 'Assess the risks they present', ['Notify each one to the HSA', 'Buy PPE for every hazard', 'Wait for an accident to happen'],
 'Section 19 requires the employer to identify the hazards, assess the risks and have a written risk assessment, which the safety statement is then based on.'),
('In the hierarchy of control, which option comes first?',
 'Eliminate the hazard', ['Issue suitable PPE', 'Put up a warning sign', 'Train the workers involved'],
 'The general principles of prevention start with avoiding the risk altogether. PPE is the last resort because it protects only the wearer and only when worn properly.'),
('Why is PPE called the last line of defence?',
 'It protects only the wearer, if used right', ['It is the cheapest control to buy', 'It removes the hazard from the site', 'The law bans it on most building sites today'],
 'PPE does nothing about the hazard itself. Removing or reducing the hazard, or guarding it, protects everyone and does not rely on the wearer.'),
('A noisy saw is replaced with a quieter model. Which type of control is this?',
 'Substitution', ['Personal protection', 'Administrative control', 'Elimination'],
 'Replacing something with a less hazardous alternative is substitution. Elimination would mean not needing the cutting at all.'),
('Putting a guard rail around a stairwell opening is an example of what?',
 'A collective protective measure', ['Personal protective equipment', 'A behaviour-based safety programme', 'A form of health surveillance'],
 'The general principles of prevention favour collective measures, which protect everyone, over individual measures such as harnesses.'),
('A site bans deliveries during the morning start, when most workers walk in through the gate. What type of control is this?',
 'An administrative control', ['An engineering control', 'Personal protective equipment', 'Elimination of the hazard'],
 'Changing how and when work is organised is an administrative control. A barrier would be an engineering control; removing vehicles altogether would be elimination.'),
('When should a risk assessment be reviewed?',
 'When work or conditions change', ['Only once every five years', 'Only after an HSA inspector visits', 'Never, once it has been signed'],
 'A risk assessment must be reviewed when there is a significant change, after an accident, or when there is reason to think it is no longer valid.'),
('A crew is about to start a task with its own method statement. What should happen first?',
 'They are briefed on it and follow it', ['They sign it without reading it', 'They file it until the HSA visits', 'They rewrite it to suit themselves'],
 'A method statement sets out the safe sequence, controls and equipment for a task. It only protects people who know what it says and follow it.'),
("What is the HSA's Safe System of Work Plan (SSWP)?",
 'A simple tick-box plan made before a task', ['A form for reporting fatal accidents to the HSA', "The HSA's annual list of inspections", 'A pension scheme for site workers'],
 'The SSWP links the safety statement to the task. It uses pictograms and ticks so a crew can plan controls before each activity, even where languages differ.'),
("Who should be involved in planning a task's safe system of work?",
 'The workers who will do the task', ['Only the client who pays for it', 'Only the HSA inspector for the area', 'Only the architect who designed it'],
 'The people doing the job know the practical hazards. Involving them makes the plan realistic and more likely to be followed.'),
('A supervisor gathers the crew for five minutes at the work face about a new hazard. What is this called?',
 'A toolbox talk', ['A site induction', 'A safety audit', 'An HSA inspection'],
 'Toolbox talks are short, focused talks on one hazard or task, usually given before work or when something changes. The induction is the first briefing for a new site.'),
('Most site accidents involve what kind of cause?',
 'Unsafe acts as well as unsafe conditions', ['Faulty equipment and nothing else', 'Bad weather in almost every case', 'Design errors made before any work starts'],
 "Behaviour-based safety recognises that people's actions, such as rushing or taking shortcuts, play a part in most accidents alongside unsafe conditions."),
('A worker removes a guard to save time. What is this an example of?',
 'An unsafe act', ['An unsafe condition', 'A design control', 'A near miss report'],
 'Removing a guard is a deliberate unsafe act. The guard being missing then becomes an unsafe condition for the next person too.'),
('A worker sees a colleague about to step under a suspended load. What should they do?',
 'Warn them straight away', ['Report it at the end of the day', 'Ignore it as it is not their trade', 'Take a photo for evidence'],
 'Every worker has a duty to protect others affected by their acts or omissions. Speaking up immediately prevents the injury.'),
('Why are shortcuts a common cause of injury on site?',
 'They skip the controls that protect workers', ['They make every job take longer', 'They are always reported to the HSA', 'They need extra paperwork afterwards for the PSCS'],
 'Shortcuts usually bypass a guard, a permit or a step in the method statement. Time pressure is a common reason people take them.'),
('A job cannot be done as the method statement says. What should the worker do?',
 'Stop and ask the supervisor', ['Find their own way to finish', 'Leave the job half done quietly', 'Ask another trade to do it'],
 'If the plan does not fit the conditions, stop and get it reviewed. Improvising can create risks nobody has assessed.'),
('Which of these jobs would most likely need a permit to work?',
 'Entering a manhole or confined space', ['Sweeping a site walkway', 'Stacking blocks on a pallet', 'Painting a site hoarding'],
 'A permit to work is a written system for high-risk tasks, setting out the precautions, isolations and checks needed before work starts and when it ends.'),
('Which human factor is most likely to lead to a mistake on a long night shift?',
 'Fatigue', ['Good lighting', 'Regular breaks', 'Clear instructions'],
 'Tiredness slows reactions and dulls judgement. Planning breaks and avoiding long hours reduce the risk of fatigue-related errors.'),
('A new worker is unsure how to use a tool safely. What should they do?',
 'Ask for instruction before using it', ['Watch a video online and start', 'Use it slowly and see how it goes', "Borrow a more experienced worker's card"],
 'Employees must use equipment in line with their training and instructions. If they have not been shown how, they should ask before starting.'),
('What does good housekeeping on site mainly prevent?',
 'Slips, trips and falls', ['Electric shocks from cables', 'Noise-induced hearing loss', 'Vibration white finger'],
 'Clear walkways and tidy work areas remove the materials and waste that people trip over, one of the most common causes of site injuries.'),
('Why do young and newly started workers need extra supervision?',
 'They are less familiar with site hazards', ['They are not covered by the 2005 Act at all', 'They cannot hold a Safe Pass card', 'They are allowed to break site rules'],
 'Inexperienced workers have higher accident rates because they may not yet recognise hazards. Employers must assess and control the risks to them.'),
]
